        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 507,
        "headingId": 373,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T15:08:51"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 461,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 374,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64534,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 507,
        "headingId": 374,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T15:08:51"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 462,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 375,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64535,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 507,
        "headingId": 375,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Sporadic tumours occur in the endocervix. Tumours arising in association with in utero diethylstilbestrol (DES) exposure occur in the ectocervix.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T15:08:51"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 463,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64536,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 507,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The clinical features are similar to those of other cervical adenocarcinomas.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T15:08:51"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64537,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 507,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These neoplasms are rare, accounting for 3&ndash;4% of cervical adenocarcinomas. They arise in two distinct settings: as sporadic tumours (median patient age: 51&nbsp;years) {18394687} and in association with in utero DES exposure {10637063} (mean patient age: 19&nbsp;years).</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T15:08:51"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64538,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 507,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "<p >This entity is not associated with high-risk HPV. Case reports of clear cell carcinomas thought by the authors to be of endocervical origin describe rare <em>POLE</em> mutation with DES exposure {30733993} and Lynch syndrome {29587389}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-07-24T14:46:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64539,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 507,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T15:08:51"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64540,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 507,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Grossly, tumours can present as endophytic with diffuse enlargement of the cervix, exophytic with a protruding mass, or without a clinically evident mass.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T15:08:51"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64541,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 507,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "<p>The tumour cells are arranged in a tubulocystic, papillary, and/or solid architecture (often admixed to varying degrees), lined by cells with clear (intracytoplasmic glycogen), eosinophilic, granular cytoplasm, sometimes hobnailed, with minimal stratification. Cytoplasmic boundaries are prominent. The nuclei show hyperchromasia, occasional enlarged nucleoli, or (less commonly) pseudonuclear inclusions. Tubules or cysts lined by a single layer of flattened epithelial cells may appear deceptively benign. The mitotic count is usually low, and stromal hyalinization is common. Endocervical glands with Arias-Stella reaction or trophoblastic disease may mimic clear cell carcinoma.</p>\r\n<p>Immunohistochemically, the tumours can be diffusely positive for p16 despite an absence of high-risk HPV infection {20727021; 21490443; 23792604; 29851704}. HNF1&beta; and napsin&nbsp;A are sensitive but nonspecific, because they may be expressed in gastric-type and HPV-associated adenocarcinomas {21490443; 29851704}. AMACR is reportedly positive in the majority of clear cell carcinomas, but it is negative in mesonephric carcinoma, which is commonly mistaken for clear cell carcinoma {31361605}. The tumours are usually negative for ER and GATA3 {21490443; 29851704}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-08-12T12:00:43"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64542,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 507,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Features characteristic of clear cell carcinoma include clear to vacuolated glycogen-rich cytoplasm, distinct cell borders, and hyaline globules. The nuclei are enlarged and hyperchromatic, with macronucleoli.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T15:08:51"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 469,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 382,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64543,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 507,
        "headingId": 382,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T15:08:51"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 472,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 385,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64544,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 507,
        "headingId": 385,
        "headingText": "<p><em>Essential:</em> a mixture of tubulocystic, papillary, and/or solid architecture; clear, eosinophilic, flat or cuboidal cells with mostly uniform but cytologically atypical nuclei, arranged in one or more patterns: tubulocystic, papillary, solid.</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em> low mitotic count; minimal cellular stratification; negative for ER and HPV.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-01T15:16:58"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 470,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 383,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64545,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 507,
        "headingId": 383,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>This entity is staged the same way as other cervical carcinomas.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T15:08:51"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 471,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 384,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64546,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 507,
        "headingId": 384,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Limited data indicate better overall survival and a lower recurrence rate in patients with stage&nbsp;I disease {18394687; 23792604}. Recurrence is usually detected within 1&nbsp;year but may occur &gt;&nbsp;3&nbsp;years after the initial surgery {18394687; 10684706}. Lymph node status is a strong predictor of overall survival and recurrence {18394687; 23792604; 10684706}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T15:08:51"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]